WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.942
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online English for Academic Purposes.

2
00:00:06.242 --> 00:00:09.664
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.964 --> 00:00:17.202
Unit four: Main Ideas and Supporting Details. Today you will separate main ideas from examples and evidence.

4
00:00:17.502 --> 00:00:20.108
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:21.067 --> 00:00:24.067
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:24.917 --> 00:00:26.034
Main idea.

7
00:00:27.050 --> 00:00:29.665
Main idea means the key point of a text.

8
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:31.646
State the main idea.

9
00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:35.332
Your turn. Say: Main idea.

10
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:39.609
Main idea.

11
00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.386
Detail.

12
00:00:42.383 --> 00:00:44.963
Detail means a small piece of information.

13
00:00:45.550 --> 00:00:47.271
Add a supporting detail.

14
00:00:47.883 --> 00:00:50.408
Your turn. Say: Detail.

15
00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:54.314
Detail.

16
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.295
Topic sentence.

17
00:00:57.283 --> 00:01:01.001
Topic sentence means a sentence that states the paragraph idea.

18
00:01:01.617 --> 00:01:03.639
Begin with a topic sentence.

19
00:01:04.250 --> 00:01:06.902
Your turn. Say: Topic sentence.

20
00:01:09.767 --> 00:01:11.046
Topic sentence.

21
00:01:11.783 --> 00:01:12.877
Example.

22
00:01:13.883 --> 00:01:16.638
Example means a case that shows an idea.

23
00:01:17.250 --> 00:01:18.390
Give an example.

24
00:01:18.983 --> 00:01:21.531
Your turn. Say: Example.

25
00:01:24.367 --> 00:01:25.379
Example.

26
00:01:26.117 --> 00:01:26.897
Support.

27
00:01:27.883 --> 00:01:30.417
Support means to give reasons for an idea.

28
00:01:31.017 --> 00:01:32.645
Evidence supports the claim.

29
00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.030
Your turn. Say: Support.

30
00:01:38.867 --> 00:01:39.833
Support.

31
00:01:40.583 --> 00:01:41.329
Claim.

32
00:01:42.317 --> 00:01:44.850
Claim means a statement that something is true.

33
00:01:45.450 --> 00:01:46.939
The writer makes a claim.

34
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.819
Your turn. Say: Claim.

35
00:01:52.667 --> 00:01:53.552
Claim.

36
00:01:54.317 --> 00:01:55.306
Conclude.

37
00:01:56.317 --> 00:01:58.514
Conclude means to decide after thinking.

38
00:01:59.117 --> 00:02:00.780
We conclude that it works.

39
00:02:01.383 --> 00:02:03.838
Your turn. Say: Conclude.

40
00:02:06.700 --> 00:02:07.689
Conclude.

41
00:02:08.450 --> 00:02:09.439
Imply.

42
00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:13.320
Imply means to suggest without saying directly.

43
00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:15.649
The text implies a problem.

44
00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.635
Your turn. Say: Imply.

45
00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:22.513
Imply.

46
00:02:23.300 --> 00:02:26.788
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:27.650 --> 00:02:29.963
Visal, how do I find the main idea?

48
00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:41.147
Look at the topic sentence, usually the first sentence of the paragraph. It tells you the point. The other sentences give details.

49
00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:43.530
What are details?

50
00:02:44.617 --> 00:02:53.622
Details are examples, facts, numbers and reasons. They support the claim. If you remove a detail, the main idea stays the same.

51
00:02:54.717 --> 00:02:56.797
What if the idea is not stated?

52
00:02:57.883 --> 00:03:07.574
Then the writer implies it. Read all the details and ask yourself what point they show together. That point is the main idea.

53
00:03:08.733 --> 00:03:10.210
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:11.050 --> 00:03:15.000
Grammar focus: Linking words for adding and exemplifying.

55
00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.557
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:21.063
Exercise improves health. For example, it protects the heart.

57
00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.276
Sleep matters. In addition, it helps memory.

58
00:03:26.233 --> 00:03:32.078
Use for example and for instance to give an example. Use in addition and moreover to add another point.

59
00:03:32.817 --> 00:03:37.094
Be careful! Don't say: For example, is not needed.

60
00:03:39.033 --> 00:03:42.194
Say: For example, it protects the heart.

61
00:03:42.494 --> 00:03:43.959
Add a full example.

62
00:03:44.767 --> 00:03:47.431
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:03:48.233 --> 00:03:49.606
Reading a Paragraph.

64
00:03:49.906 --> 00:03:52.741
Bopha reads a paragraph about healthy habits.

65
00:03:53.733 --> 00:03:56.569
The first sentence says that exercise improves health.

66
00:03:57.567 --> 00:04:00.936
The next sentences give examples such as a stronger heart.

67
00:04:01.933 --> 00:04:04.537
She sees that these details support the main idea.

68
00:04:05.533 --> 00:04:08.055
She writes the main idea in her own words.

69
00:04:09.167 --> 00:04:12.971
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:13.817 --> 00:04:15.851
A topic sentence states the blank.

71
00:04:16.151 --> 00:04:20.292
A, author's age. B, main idea. C, page number.

72
00:04:23.833 --> 00:04:26.262
The answer is B: main idea.

73
00:04:27.417 --> 00:04:29.068
Details blank the claim.

74
00:04:29.368 --> 00:04:32.615
A, delete. B, paint. C, support.

75
00:04:36.167 --> 00:04:38.352
The answer is C: support.

76
00:04:39.517 --> 00:04:41.551
For example introduces an blank.

77
00:04:41.851 --> 00:04:45.214
A, example. B, ending. C, title.

78
00:04:48.767 --> 00:04:51.091
The answer is A: example.

79
00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:54.261
Imply means to suggest blank.

80
00:04:54.561 --> 00:04:58.563
A, twice. B, indirectly. C, loudly.

81
00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:04.076
The answer is B: indirectly.

82
00:05:05.217 --> 00:05:07.878
Bopha writes the main idea in her blank words.

83
00:05:08.178 --> 00:05:11.286
A, teacher's. B, friend's. C, own.

84
00:05:14.833 --> 00:05:16.752
The answer is C: own.

85
00:05:17.967 --> 00:05:21.374
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:22.217 --> 00:05:24.019
How do you find the main idea?

87
00:05:32.817 --> 00:05:34.340
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:49.520
I look for the topic sentence, usually at the start of the paragraph. Then I check that the other sentences give details that support it. If it is not stated, I decide what point all the details show.

89
00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:51.739
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:05:52.650 --> 00:05:57.798
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and linking words for adding and exemplifying.

91
00:05:58.098 --> 00:06:02.504
Today's value is analytical thinking. Look for the pattern underneath the surface.

92
00:06:02.804 --> 00:06:05.561
See you in Unit five. Goodbye!

93
00:06:05.861 --> 00:06:08.745
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

